- How would you extend this to detect unsatisfiable sets efficiently?
- How does adding a priority among constraints affect the search strategy?

## Why This Is Asked

To evaluate ability to design minimal-witness solvers for Horn-like rules and detect derivability with backtracking.

## Key Concepts

- Horn clauses
- Minimal witness extraction
- Forward-chaining with backtracking
- Cycle detection in derivations

## Code Example

```javascript
// simple forward-chaining for (A∧B)→C rules
function canDerive(target, base, rules){
  const derived = new Set(base);
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const [A,B,C] of rules){
      if (derived.has(A) && derived.has(B) && !derived.has(C)){
        derived.add(C);
        changed = true;
      }
    }
  }
  return derived.has(target);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to arbitrary antecedent sizes or negative literals?
- How would you enumerate all minimal witnesses or detect unsatisfiable cycles in large graphs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason with Kleene 3-valued logic in a realistic policy-engine style, including short-circuit semantics and propagation.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic: ¬ flips T/F, U→U; ∧/∨ follow min/max with T>U>F; A→B as ¬A ∨ B.
- Short-circuit evaluation in rule engines.
- Backtracking propagation for constraint satisfaction in a 3-valued setting.

## Code Example
```javascript
function kleeneNot(a){ return a === 'T' ? 'F' : a === 'F' ? 'T' : 'U' }
function kleeneOr(a,b){ /* min/max with T>U>F */ return (a === 'T' || b === 'T') ? 'T' : (a === 'U' || b === 'U') ? 'U' : 'F' }
function kleeneAnd(a,b){ return (a === 'F' || b === 'F') ? 'F' : (a === 'U' || b === 'U') ? 'U' : 'T' }
function implies(a,b){ // ¬a ∨ b
  const na = kleeneNot(a)
  return kleeneOr(na, b)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does the answer change if short-circuiting is disabled? 
- How would you extend to detect unsatisfiable sets with more variables?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical logical deduction and simple SAT handling in a controlled, small-domain setting.

## Key Concepts
- Implication with negation
- Disjunction
- Satisfiability search
- Backtracking basics
- Early termination on first valid assignment

## Code Example
```javascript
function isSat(A,B) {
  // Clause1: (!A => B)  == (A || B)
  // Clause2: (B => A)   == (!B || A)
  // Clause3: (A || B)
  return (A || B) && (!B || A) && (A || B);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to three variables with similar clauses?
- How does short-circuit evaluation affect performance in larger clause sets?

## Why This Is Asked

Temporal-like constraints on feature flags across release cycles test three-valued propagation and branching heuristics—skills essential for configuration validation at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic with implication defined as ¬A ∨ B
- Propagation rules for ¬, ∨, ∧, and →
- Backtracking search treating U as a first-class domain value

## Code Example

```javascript
// Three-valued backtracking propagator sketch
const VALUES = { T: 2, U: 1, F: 0 };
const NOT = { T: 'F', F: 'T', U: 'U' };
const OR = (a, b) => VALUES[a] >= VALUES[b] ? a : b;
const AND = (a, b) => VALUES[a] <= VALUES[b] ? a : b;
const IMPLIES = (a, b) => OR(NOT[a], b);

function propagate(constraints, assignment) {
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const [ante, conseq] of constraints) {
      const a = assignment[ante], c = assignment[conseq];
      if (a === 'F' && c !== 'F') { assignment[conseq] = 'F'; changed = true; }
      if (a === 'T' && c !== 'T') { assignment[conseq] = 'T'; changed = true; }
      if (c === 'F' && a !== 'F') { assignment[ante] = 'F'; changed = true; }
    }
  }
  return assignment;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How does the branching heuristic affect worst-case complexity?
- How would you extend this to N time steps?

## Why This Is Asked
